<TopicPage slug="subjects/forestry/foundational/forests-and-forestry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Forestry is the management of long-lived ecosystems for multiple, competing values: wood,
    water, wildlife, carbon and people. Unlike most land uses, the crop takes decades to grow — so
    a forester's decisions today describe a forest someone else will harvest. That time horizon,
    plus the fact that forests are habitats as much as resources, is what makes the discipline
    distinctive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="what forests provide — the value ledger" widget="equationStep" config={valuesCfg} />

  <h2>The global picture: stabilising but uneven</h2>
  <p>
    The world's forest area has stopped shrinking overall — FAO's assessments show the decline
    slowing from over 10 million hectares lost per year in the 1990s to a small residual net loss,
    with gains in temperate regions offsetting losses in the tropics. The global number hides the
    story: primary tropical forest keeps falling while plantations and regrowth rise.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="global forest area, 1990–2020 (FAO FRA)" widget="plotter" config={faoCfg} />

  <h2>Sustained yield: the founding idea</h2>
  <p>
    Modern forestry began in 18th-century Germany with a simple accounting idea: harvest no more
    wood per year than the forest grows. Sustained yield made forestry a science of rotations —
    plant, tend, harvest, regenerate — and later broadened into sustained yield of <em>everything</em>
    a forest provides. Its modern descendants are certification schemes and multi-criteria
    management plans.
  </p>

  <h2>What foresters actually do</h2>
  <p>
    The working cycle is: know the trees (identification and silvics), measure the forest
    (mensuration and inventory), decide the treatment (silviculture), plan across decades
    (management), and defend the result (protection from fire, pests and disease). Behind all of it
    stands ecology — because a managed forest is still an ecosystem, run on sunlight and soil.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SAF accreditation standards; FAO Global Forest Resources Assessment 2020; Nyland, <em>Silviculture: Concepts and Applications</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
